From e460850db297df44dccb57fb4e5d8e122275bd59 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: c6 <c6@localhost>
Date: Fri, 6 Nov 2026 20:42:51 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 10/10] c6: structured interfaces, no 'impl' declaration

A struct implements an interface when it has the methods the interface
declares.  The 'impl IFACE for TYPE { ... }' block is gone: methods are
ordinary functions (see the method commit) and are found by the name
they were declared with, so 'interface Shape { int area(); }' is
satisfied by 'int area(struct Circle *self)'.

The check is lazy: the first conversion from a struct to an interface
value (a call argument, an assignment or an explicit cast) requires that
every interface method is declared before that point and that its
signature matches the vtable slot, otherwise the diagnostic names the
missing or mismatching method.  The vtable is a 'static const' object
that is emitted at the end of the unit for every pair actually used, so
two units may convert the same type without clashing.  Emission runs
after inline bodies are compiled and repeats while new conversions turn
up, since a 'static inline' body is only compiled at the end of the
unit and may contain a conversion itself.

Because the interface name is now a plain typedef and 'impl' is gone,
'impl' is an ordinary identifier again and a struct cannot silently
inherit an interface it does not implement.  Interface to interface
assignment stays a plain copy; file scope interface initializers remain
unsupported, as before.

The compiler also drops its per-unit state (imports, interfaces,
methods, pending vtables) when a new translation unit starts: the
scanner restarts its token table for every unit, so keeping the tokens
would leak stale imports and interfaces into the next unit.  'test'
entries are deliberately kept, since '-test' collects them across units.

Tests: 160 rewritten without 'impl' (plus a conversion inside a static
inline body), 162 checks the new diagnostics, 174 converts the same type
in two units.  tests2 153/153 with C6_EXT, 134/134 without.
